{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Professional Learning Network","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting professional learning network to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"07","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_professional_learning_network","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Professional Learning Network\n\n![Professional Learning Network textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-07-professional-learning-network.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA professional learning network, the colleagues, online groups, and shared resources you draw on, is a source, not an authority. The distinction matters because networks circulate popularity, and popularity is not evidence. A tool recommended by 300 teachers in a Facebook group has 300 recommendations and possibly zero studies; the count measures reach, not effectiveness, and certainly not fit with your placement. Treating the network as a source means asking what backs each claim and whether the recommender's context resembles yours, before adopting anything.\n\nThis section treats the professional learning network as something you vet and contribute to, not something you follow. The skill is source evaluation under social pressure: separating signal from popularity, checking context fit, and contributing your own findings in a form colleagues can actually judge.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish popularity (how many recommend a tool) from evidence (what backs the recommendation).\n- Check whether a recommender's context (grade, subject, device ratio) resembles your placement before adopting.\n- Verify AI-supplied citations and treat unlocatable sources as likely fabricated.\n- Contribute findings back in a form colleagues can judge for fit, including where a strategy did not work.\n\n## Opening Case\n\n\"300 teachers use it\" is a social proof signal, and social proof is a heuristic that bypasses evaluation. It feels like evidence because agreement is comfortable, but the number tells you only that the tool spread, not that it worked, and not that it works in a room like yours. A tool can be popular because it is genuinely good, or because it is free, well-marketed, or fun regardless of learning outcomes.\n\nThe vetting move is to ask what the 300 recommendations are made of. Do the recommenders teach your grade and subject? What evidence backs their claims beyond enthusiasm? What does the tool do with student data? A recommendation from a one-to-one device school says little about your shared-cart placement. The network surfaced the tool; your job is to test it against your context before you trust it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nSource evaluation in a network rests on three filters: evidence, context, and provenance. The **evidence filter** asks what supports a claim beyond the fact that someone made it; a strategy described with outcomes and conditions is stronger than one described with enthusiasm. The **context filter** asks whether the recommender's situation resembles yours; a glowing report from a 1-to-1 school is a different claim in a shared-cart placement, and the gap must be tested, not assumed away. The **provenance filter** asks where a cited fact actually comes from; in an age of AI-generated summaries, a citation that cannot be located is more likely fabricated than obscure.\n\nThese filters convert the network from an authority you defer to into a source you interrogate. The same skills make you a better contributor: when you post back, you supply the evidence, the context, and the locatable sources that let colleagues run the same three filters on your claim.\n\nMatch each red flag in a viral recommendation to the filter that catches it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"The recommender teaches a 1-to-1 device school; your placement shares one cart\",\"definition\":\"Context filter (does the recommender situation resemble yours?)\"},{\"term\":\"An AI summary cites three studies that no search can locate\",\"definition\":\"Provenance filter (where does the cited fact actually come from?)\"},{\"term\":\"The only support offered is that 300 teachers love it\",\"definition\":\"Evidence filter (what backs the claim beyond enthusiasm?)\"},{\"term\":\"The 20 percent score gain has no comparison group or time period\",\"definition\":\"Evidence filter (outcomes and conditions, not just a number)\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThree lenses sharpen the filters:\n\n- **Critical edtech studies** asks whose interests a popular tool serves; virality often tracks marketing budget or novelty rather than learning, and the lens keeps you from mistaking reach for quality.\n- **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** is the direct source of the provenance problem: AI summaries of \"best practices\" routinely cite studies that do not exist, so any AI-supplied citation must be located before it is trusted.\n- **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** supplies the context filter's substance; whether a tool fits depends on the specific intersection of your content, your pedagogy, and your device reality, which a recommender's context may not share.\n\nA lens has done its work when it changed whether or how you adopted a recommendation, not just how you described it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"'300 teachers use it, so it must be effective for my placement.' What does the count of 300 actually measure?\" explanation=\"It measures reach, not effectiveness, and certainly not fit with your placement. A tool can be popular because it is free, well-marketed, or fun regardless of learning outcomes. Social proof is a heuristic that bypasses evaluation; the vetting move is to ask what the 300 recommendations are made of.\" options='[{\"text\":\"How far the tool spread, not whether it works in a room like yours\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"That the tool is effective, since 300 teachers cannot be wrong\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"That the tool fits your grade, subject, and device ratio\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two responses to the same viral recommendation. The popularity-as-proof version skips evaluation:\n\n> 300 teachers use it, so it must be effective for my placement.\n\nThis treats reach as evidence and ignores context entirely. The vetted version runs the filters:\n\n> I checked what evidence backs the claims, whether the recommenders teach my grade and subject, and what the tool does with student data. A colleague's glowing strategy came from a 1-to-1 device school; my placement shares one cart, so I will test the strategy under my constraint before adopting it. An AI summary of best practices cited three studies; none appeared when I searched for them, so I treated the citations as likely fabricated and used only sources I could locate.\n\nThe second version surfaces a context gap (one-to-one versus shared cart) and a provenance failure (three uncited \"studies\") that the popularity version would have carried straight into the classroom. The contribution that follows posts what was tried, where it worked and where it did not, and a caution about the shared-device constraint, so colleagues can judge fit rather than just reshare.\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Vetting a Viral Tool From a Teacher Network\n\nLena finds a viral AI lesson-plan generator recommended by 300 teachers in a Facebook group.\n\n1. **Before (popularity as proof):** \"300 teachers use it, so it must be effective for my placement.\"\n2. **Vet the source:** She checks what evidence backs the claims, whether the recommenders teach her grade and subject, and what the tool does with student data.\n3. **Context gap named:** A colleague's glowing strategy came from a 1-to-1 device school; Lena's placement shares one cart. She records the gap and plans to test the strategy under her constraint before adopting it.\n4. **AI citations verified:** An AI summary of \"best practices\" cited three studies. None appeared when she searched for them, so she treated the citations as likely fabricated and used only sources she could locate.\n5. **After (contribution back to the network):** She posts what she tried, the context where it worked and where it did not, and a caution about the shared-device constraint, so colleagues can judge fit rather than just reshare.\n\nThe judgment is legible: the network was a source she vetted, not an authority she followed.\n\nNow vet a viral tool yourself. Each turn either runs a filter or lets popularity carry the claim into your classroom.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Vetting a viral AI lesson-plan generator\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"A free AI lesson-plan generator is recommended by 300 teachers in a Facebook group. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt it; 300 teachers cannot be wrong\",\"next\":\"popularity\",\"feedback\":\"You treated reach as evidence and skipped evaluation.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the evidence filter: ask what backs the claim beyond the count\",\"next\":\"evidence\",\"feedback\":\"You interrogated the source instead of deferring to it.\"}]},\"popularity\":{\"prompt\":\"You adopt on popularity. A colleague glowing review came from a 1-to-1 school; your placement shares one cart, and the tool stalls. What was skipped?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The context filter; reach said nothing about fit\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"A one-to-one success can be a shared-cart failure.\"}]},\"restart\":{\"prompt\":\"Restart and run the filters before adopting.\",\"feedback\":\"Adopting on popularity alone means you followed the network rather than vetting it.\",\"choices\":[]},\"evidence\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague glowing strategy came from a 1-to-1 device school. Your placement shares one cart. What next?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Assume it will transfer; a good strategy is a good strategy\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"You assumed away the context gap instead of testing it.\"},{\"label\":\"Record the gap and plan to test the strategy under your constraint first\",\"next\":\"provenance\",\"feedback\":\"You ran the context filter and refused to assume the gap away.\"}]},\"provenance\":{\"prompt\":\"An AI summary of best practices cites three studies. You search and none appear. What do you conclude?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"They are probably obscure; cite them anyway\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"An unlocatable citation is more likely fabricated than obscure.\"},{\"label\":\"Treat them as likely fabricated and use only sources you can locate\",\"next\":\"contribute\",\"feedback\":\"You ran the provenance filter and discarded the unverifiable citations.\"}]},\"contribute\":{\"prompt\":\"You tested the tool. How do you give back to the network?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reshare it as a success so others can benefit\",\"next\":\"restart\",\"feedback\":\"An unconditioned success story just adds to the noise the filters exist to cut.\"},{\"label\":\"Post what you tried, where it worked and where it did not, and the shared-cart caution\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"You contributed a claim colleagues can run the same filters on.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"You ran evidence, context, and provenance, then contributed with conditions attached. The network was a source you vetted.\",\"feedback\":\"Popularity was separated from evidence, the context gap was tested, and only locatable sources survived.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun evidence, context, and provenance before you adopt, and contribute back in a form that lets others run the same filters. The order is: ask what backs the claim, check whether the recommender's context matches yours, locate every cited source, then post your own findings with their conditions attached. Lena follows it: she tests evidence and context, refuses the unlocatable citations, and contributes a caution rather than a reshare.\n\nBefore you adopt from a network, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Evidence check:** Beyond how many people recommend it, what actually supports the claim that this tool helps learning?\n2. **Context check:** Does the recommender's grade, subject, and device reality resemble yours, and have you tested the gap if it does not?\n3. **Provenance check:** Did you locate every cited source, and did you discard the AI-supplied citations you could not find?\n\nIf you adopted on popularity alone, you followed the network rather than vetting it.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating a recommendation count as an effectiveness measure; reach and quality are different things, and virality often tracks marketing rather than learning. The second is assuming a strategy that worked elsewhere will work in your context; a one-to-one success can be a shared-cart failure, and the gap must be tested. The third is trusting AI-supplied citations without locating them; when a summary cites three studies that no search can find, the honest conclusion is fabrication, not obscurity, and only locatable sources should survive into your reasoning.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the three filters to a different network claim. A colleague in a teacher Slack posts that a particular AI flashcard app \"raised our test scores 20 percent.\" Run the filters. Evidence: ask what the 20 percent is measured against, over what period, and whether anything else changed at the same time; a number with no comparison group is a story, not a finding. Context: this colleague teaches an honors section with one-to-one devices, while your placement is a mixed-level class sharing a cart, so the result may not transfer. Provenance: if the colleague attributes the claim to \"research,\" ask for the study and try to locate it; if it traces back to the vendor's own marketing page, weight it accordingly.\n\nThe vetted conclusion is not \"ignore it\" but \"test it small, under my conditions, and watch for the access barrier the shared cart creates.\" Then you contribute: you post your own result with its conditions, including if it underperformed, so the next teacher inherits a vettable claim rather than another unconditioned success story. The walkthrough is the same structure every time, applied to a new claim.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a network recommendation, keep a two-column note: **what is claimed** on the left, **what backs it** on the right. A recommendation with a full left column and an empty right column is popularity dressed as evidence. The presence of locatable sources, matched context, and stated conditions on the right is the signal that a claim is worth acting on.\n\nA strong reader of a network claim can answer four questions:\n\n1. What evidence beyond popularity supports this recommendation?\n2. Does the recommender's context resemble mine, and have I tested the gap?\n3. Are the cited sources locatable, or are they likely AI-fabricated?\n4. If I contributed this back, would a colleague have enough to judge fit, including where it failed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA network contribution is strong when a colleague reading it could decide whether the strategy fits their own room without contacting you. That requires stating the context you tried it in, the evidence of what happened, the conditions under which it worked, and honestly where it did not. Polish is the last layer; a contribution that reads as an enthusiastic endorsement but omits its context and its failures has added to the noise the three filters exist to cut through.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like this: \"I am posting this with its conditions because a recommendation without context just spreads; it worked in my shared-cart room only after I pre-loaded the devices, and it did not help my multilingual learners. I dropped the AI-cited studies I could not locate, because an unverifiable source is not support.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same vetting discipline to a higher-stakes channel: a district professional-development session where an outside vendor presents a tool as research-backed to a room of teachers who will be expected to adopt it. The social pressure is now institutional, not just a group thread. The three filters still apply, but running them means asking the vendor, in a room, for the studies, the test population, and the data-handling policy, which takes more standing than questioning a Facebook post. If you can run evidence, context, and provenance against an authoritative presenter, you understand the network skill as professional judgment rather than online skepticism.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-07-professional-learning-network-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 07.07. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_professional_learning_network\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that you asked what backs a recommendation beyond popularity, tested whether the recommender's context matches yours, located every cited source, and contributed back with conditions a colleague could judge. That is what makes a network a source rather than an authority.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_professional_learning_network\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_professional_learning_network","stem":"A candidate finds a viral AI lesson-plan generator recommended by 300 teachers in a Facebook group. Which note shows she is treating her professional learning network as a source to vet rather than an authority to follow?","options":["300 teachers use it, so it must be effective for my placement.","The post is popular; before I use it I will check what evidence backs the claims, whether the recommenders teach my grade and subject, and what the tool does with student data.","It has the most likes in the group, so I will adopt it this week.","A teacher I admire shared it, so the data question does not matter."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A professional learning network is a source to vet. Defensible practice checks the evidence, the relevance of the recommenders, and the data handling, rather than treating popularity or admiration as proof.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_popularity_as_evidence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_professional_learning_network","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A network colleague shares a strategy that worked great in their affluent suburban school. Which annotation best protects the candidate's judgment as she considers it for her under-resourced placement?","options":["It worked for them, so it should work for me.","Note the context gap: their 1-to-1 device school differs from my shared-cart placement, so I must test whether the strategy survives that constraint before adopting it.","Note that the colleague is more experienced than I am.","Note that the strategy looks impressive in their photos."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Borrowing from a network requires checking whether the source context matches the placement. Naming the context gap is the judgment move; deference or appearances are not.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_borrow_without_context_check","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate asks an AI tool to summarize the best practices from her online teacher network. The AI cites three studies, but none of them appear when she searches for them. What should she do?","options":["Share the AI summary with the network because it sounds authoritative.","Stop and treat the unverifiable citations as a signal the AI may have fabricated them, then rely only on sources she can locate and check.","Assume the studies exist but are hard to find.","Add the citations to her own lesson plan to look rigorous."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Citations that cannot be located are a fabrication signal. A teacher who shares them into her network would spread misinformation. Defensible practice verifies sources before passing them on.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_ai_citation_unverified","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_professional_learning_network_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"What record best shows the candidate added value to her professional learning network rather than just resharing?","options":["A count of how many posts she liked.","A note of the strategy she tried, the context where it worked and where it did not, and the caution she passed back so colleagues can judge fit.","The number of followers she gained.","A list of every tool mentioned in the group."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Contributing to a network means reporting tested evidence and context-bound cautions others can use. Engagement metrics or a tool list do not show professional contribution.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_resharing_as_contribution","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_popularity_as_evidence","pattern":"popularity_as_evidence","description":"Treating how many teachers recommend a tool as proof it is effective or safe","trigger":"candidate adopts a resource because it is popular or admired, skipping the evidence and data check","feedback":"Treat the network as a source to vet. Check the evidence, the recommenders' relevance, and the tool's data handling.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_borrow_without_context_check","pattern":"borrow_without_context_check","description":"Adopting a colleague's strategy without checking whether their context matches the placement","trigger":"candidate borrows a strategy from a different school context without naming the gap","feedback":"Name the context gap between the source and your placement, then test whether the strategy survives your constraints.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_ai_citation_unverified","pattern":"ai_citation_unverified","description":"Sharing AI-provided citations into a network without verifying they exist","trigger":"candidate passes on study citations she could not locate","feedback":"Unverifiable citations are a fabrication signal. Rely only on sources you can locate and check before sharing them.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_7_resharing_as_contribution","pattern":"resharing_as_contribution","description":"Treating likes, follows, or reshares as a professional contribution to the network","trigger":"candidate reports engagement metrics instead of tested evidence and context cautions","feedback":"Contribute by reporting what you tried, where it worked and did not, and the caution colleagues need to judge fit.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"83070be17f0b56a70e9a7afeab23e434a5c55a4466c251c1c8110d4fc4e6e623","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}